{"amendments":[{"body":"crates/tank_core/src/types/budget.rs:18-50, crates/tank_core/src/types/budget.rs:109-115, and crates/tank_core/src/engine.rs:91-149 collapse each stage to `after - before` via `ElementBudget::from_delta`, so `in_mg`/`out_mg` are only the sign-split net change, not the actual gross source and sink terms. That breaks on stages that both remove and add the same element in one pass, such as `action:water_change` in crates/tank_core/src/systems/water_change.rs:44-82 when the replacement profile is non-zero, and `system:dissolved_oxygen` in crates/tank_core/src/systems/dissolved_oxygen.rs:31-48 where reaeration/photosynthesis offset respiration. In those cases the ledger can report `0` or only a small net delta even though a real export/import or oxygen demand occurred, so it does not satisfy the requirement to record per-element sources and sinks during a tick.","rationale":"This is a correctness gap in the new ledger model: it hides exactly the mixed-flow cases the instrumentation is supposed to explain."},{"body":"crates/tank_core/tests/budget_tracking.rs:163-193 and crates/tank_core/tests/budget_tracking.rs:258-293 do not actually guard future element-bearing fields. The test only compares helper labels against paths admitted by the hard-coded `is_nitrogen_budget_path` / `is_carbon_budget_path` allowlists, so adding a new explicit N/C pool to `TankState` will still leave the test green unless the allowlist is manually updated in the same patch. That defeats the bead's requirement that later phases extending state should trip a guardrail if `total_nitrogen()` / `total_carbon()` are not extended.","rationale":"This is a significant maintainability risk because the canonical conservation helpers can silently fall out of sync with the state schema."}],"summary":"Reviewed the budget-tracking implementation and its targeted tests. I found one substantive correctness issue in how the ledger records gross in/out flows, plus one future-proofing hole in the helper-coverage test that can let new element-bearing pools go uncounted."}